      "text": "To find the maximum value of \\(\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2}\\), we start by expanding the numerator:\n\n\\[\n(x + y)^2 = x^2 + 2xy + y^2.\n\\]\n\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{x^2 + 2xy + y^2}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\n\nWe can separate this into three fractions:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2 + 2xy + y^2}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{x^2}{x^2 + y^2} + \\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2} + \\frac{y^2}{x^2 + y^2}.\n\\]\n\nTo find the maximum value, we will analyze each term separately. First, consider the term \\(\\frac{x^2}{x^2 + y^2}\\). This term is always less than 1 because \\(x^2 < x^2 + y^2\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{x^2 + y^2} < 1.\n\\]\n\nNext, consider the term \\(\\frac{y^2}{x^2 + y^2}\\). This term is also always less than 1 because \\(y^2 < x^2 + y^2\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{x^2 + y^2} < 1.\n\\]\n\nNow, consider the term \\(\\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2}\\). We can use the identity \\(x^2 + y^2 = (x + y)^2 - 2xy\\) to rewrite it:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2xy}{x^2 + y^2} = \\frac{2xy}{(x + y)^2 - 2xy}.\n\\]\n\nTo simplify further, let \\(t = \\frac{x}{y}\\). Then \\(x = ty\\) and the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(ty + y)^2}{(ty)^2 + y^2} = \\frac{(t + 1)^2 y^2}{t^2 y^2 + y^2} = \\frac{(t + 1)^2}{t^2 + 1}.\n\\]\n\nWe need to maximize \\(\\frac{t + 1}{t^2 + 1}\\). To do this, we can use the AM-GM inequality:\n\n\\[\nt^2 + 1 \\geq 2t \\implies \\frac{t + 1}{t^2 + 1} \\leq \\frac{t + 1}{2t}.\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the maximum value of \\(\\frac{t + 1}{2t}\\). Let \\(f(t) = \\frac{t + 1}{2t}\\). Then:\n\n\\[\nf(t) = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2t}.\n\\]\n\nThe function \\(\\frac{1}{2t}\\) is decreasing for \\(t > 0\\), so the maximum value of \\(f(t)\\) occurs at the smallest positive value of \\(t\\), which is \\(t = 1\\):\n\n\\[\nf(1) = \\frac{1 + 1}{2 \\cdot 1} = 1.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of \\(\\frac{(x + y)^2}{x^2 + y^2}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}.\n\\]",
